    "title": "512BB-Powered Ferrari 412P Re-Creation by Norwood Autocraft",
    "summary": "This Ferrari 412P re-creation was built by marque restoration specialist Bob Norwood in early 1982 using the shortened chassis of a wrecked Ferrari 365 GT4 BB and fiberglass body panels that were reportedly formed using spare Ferrari P3 alloy panels as bucks. The build included the installation of the 4.9-liter F102B flat-12 and five-speed manual transaxle from a Ferrari 512 BB along with period Ferrari running gear. After its completion, the car was highlighted on the cover of a 1982 issue of Petersen’s Sports Car Classics magazine and was subsequently sold to the founder of the Dallas Grand Prix, where it was displayed in 1984. By 1990, it had made its way to the UK, where it spent time under the ownership of Lord Charles Brocket. After spending a decade under Belgian ownership, the car returned to the US and remained under the stewardship of its previous owner until his 2024 passing. Finished in red, the car features quad Weber carburetors, four-wheel hydraulic disc brakes, coilover independent suspension, staggered-width 15” white-paint Campagnolo wheels, fixed-back aluminum seats trimmed in black upholstery, and four-point harnesses. This 412P re-creation is now offered on dealer consignment in Costa Mesa, California, with historical documents and photos and a Montana title describing it as a 1979 Ferrari P4.",
